  "root_cause": {
    "mechanism": "OpenProcess + GetProcessTimes succeed on a terminated, handle-referenced process object; Windows frees the object only after exit AND full handle release.",
    "proven_by": [
      "probe_win_exit case A",
      "probe_real_topology case 2",
      "before_after",
      "tests test_x02",
      "tests test_x03"
    ],
    "statement": "process_alive() is not an exit test on Windows. It answers 'is this PID still addressable?', which remains true for an already-exited process for as long as ANY handle to the process object is open. _await_exit() polled exactly that, so a console the kernel had already reported as terminated was reported to the owner as still running, and the full 15 s budget was consumed before CONSOLE_DID_NOT_STOP.",
    "unproven_and_declared": {
      "surviving_explanations": [
        "third-party handle holder (AV / indexer / WMI / conhost)",
        "genuinely slow kernel-side process teardown"
      ],
      "which_component_held_the_handle_on_the_owner_machine": "UNPROVEN",
      "why_it_does_not_matter": "The launcher's own topology releases in 0.25 s on this machine (probe_real_topology case 1), so a third party held the reference, or the kernel teardown was genuinely slow. The accepted baseline cannot distinguish those two, and that inability IS the defect. Exit is now proven through a handle the launcher holds itself, so neither explanation can produce a false CONSOLE_DID_NOT_STOP."
    },
    "verdict": "PROVEN"
  },
